Under IFRS (IAS 2), the notes cover:

- the **accounting policy**, including the cost formula used;
- the total carrying amount, broken down by **classification** such as raw materials, work in progress and finished goods;
- the amount of inventory recognised as an **expense** (COGS) in the period;
- the amount of any **write-downs** to net realisable value, and any **reversals** with the reason for them;
- inventory **pledged as security** for liabilities;
- inventory carried at fair value less costs to sell, if any.

US GAAP requires similar information. A US firm using **LIFO** (last-in, first-out) typically also discloses the **LIFO reserve**. This is the gap between what inventory would be under FIFO (first-in, first-out) and what is reported under LIFO. Some companies disclose it as a separate line on the balance sheet. LIFO is not permitted under IFRS, so IFRS reporters use FIFO, weighted average or specific identification.

The notes are only one source. To judge whether an inventory build is harmless (for example, stocking up before a product launch) or a problem, also read:

- the **management discussion and analysis (MD&A)** and earnings-call remarks about demand, pricing and supply;
- **segment data**, to see which business is building stock;
- **industry and competitor data**, such as peer inventory days and sector sales trends;
- the **cash flow statement**, to see how much cash is tied up in inventory.

An unqualified audit opinion gives comfort about fair presentation, but it does not tell you whether the stock can be sold at a profit. Use the numbers to ask questions, then look for answers in the narrative and external data.
